e><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Environmental variation shapes sexual dimorphism in red deer.</article-title><source>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America</source><volume>96</volume><fpage>4467</fpage><lpage>4471</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10200285</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-WarbrickSmith1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Warbrick-Smith</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Behmer</surname><given-names>ST</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>KP</given-names></name><name><surname>Raubenheimer</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Simpson</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Evolving resistance to obesity in an insect.</article-title><source>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America</source><volume>103</volume><fpage>14045</fpage><lpage>14049</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16968774</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Mays1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mays</surname><given-names>DL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1971</year><article-title>Mating Behavior of Nemobiine Crickets: <italic>Hygronemobius, Nemobius, and Pteronemobius</italic> (Orthoptera: Gryllidae).</article-title><source>Florida Entomologist</source><volume>54</volume><fpage>113</fpage><lpage>126</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Fedorka1"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fedorka</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Mousseau</surname><given-names>TA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Tibial spur feeding in ground crickets: Larger males contribute larger gifts (Orthoptera : Gryllidae).</article-title><source>Florida Entomologist</source><volume>85</volume><fpage>317</fpage><lpage>323</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Fedorka2"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fedorka</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Mousseau</surname><given-names>TA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Material and genetic benefits of female multiple mating and polyandry.</article-title><source>Animal Behaviour</source><volume>64</volume><fpage>361</fpage><lpage>367</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Gwynne1"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gwynne</surname><given-names>DT</given-names></name></person-group><year>1982</year><article-title>Mate selection by female katydids (Orthoptera: Tettigoniidae, <italic>Conocephalus nigropleurum</italic>).</article-title><source>Animal Behaviour</source><volume>30</volume><fpage>734</fpage><lpage>738</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-DeLuca1"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>De Luca</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>GK</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Courtship communication in meadow katydids: female preference for large male vibrations.</article-title><source>Behaviour</source><volume>135</volume><fpage>777</fpage><lpage>794</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Brown1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>WD</given-names></name><name><surname>Wideman</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Andrade</surname><given-names>MCB</given-names></name><name><surname>Mason</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Gwynne</surname><given-names>DT</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>Female choice for an indicator of male size in the song of the black-horned tree cricket Oecanthus nigricornis (Orthoptera: Gryllidae: Oecanthinae).</article-title><source>Evolution</source><volume>50</volume><fpage>2400</fpage><lpage>2411</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Fedorka3"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fedorka</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Mousseau</surname><given-names>TA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Nuptial gifts and the evolution of male body size.</article-title><source>Evolution</source><volume>56</volume><fpage>590</fpage><lpage>596</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11989688</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Simmons1"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Simmons</surname><given-names>LW</given-names></name><name><surname>Gwynne</surname><given-names>DT</given-names></name></person-group><year>1993</year><article-title>Reproductive investment in bushcrickets: the allocation of male and female nutrients to offspring.</article-title><source>Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences</source><volume>252</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>5</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003437-Simmons2"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Simmons</surname><given-names>LW</given-names></name></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>Reproductive energetics of the role reversing bush-cricket, <italic>Kawanaphila nartee</italic> (Orthoptera: Tettigoniidae: Zaprochilinae).</article-title><source>Journal of Evolutionary Biology</source><volume>7</volume><fpage>189</fpage><lpage>200</lpag